An enrollment code for one of your service accounts is about to expire. Once
it does, `ssoossh service retrieve` stops issuing certificates for it and any
unattended job that renews through it starts failing.

{{ if .Daily }}You will hear about this every day until the code expires.{{ else }}You will hear about this again in a week, then every day over its final week.{{ end }}

WHAT IT HANDS OUT

  Principal             {{ if .ServiceAccount }}{{ .ServiceAccount }}{{ else }}{{ join .Principals ", " }}{{ end }}
  Key ID                {{ .KeyID }}
  Bound key             {{ .PublicKeyType }} {{ .PublicKeyFingerprint }}

THE CODE ITSELF

  Code expires          {{ datetime .CodeExpiresAt }} ({{ remaining .CodeExpiresAt }})
  First redeemed        {{ if .FirstRedeemedAt.IsZero }}never{{ else }}{{ datetime .FirstRedeemedAt }}{{ end }}
  Enrollment ID         {{ .EnrollmentID }}
{{ if .RequestID }}  Request ID            {{ .RequestID }}
{{ end }}
{{ if .FirstRedeemedAt.IsZero }}This code has never been redeemed, which usually means the job it was created
for was never finished. Letting it expire is then the right outcome.{{ else }}This code has been in use, so something is very likely still renewing through
it.{{ end }}

TO KEEP THE JOB RUNNING

Run `ssoossh service enroll` again on the host that holds the key and have the
request approved. That mints a new code; the old one keeps working until it
expires, so there is no gap if you do it before the date above.

Certificates already issued from this code are unaffected. They stay valid for
their own lifetimes, which is why a job can keep running for a while after the
code behind it has expired.
